<problem>
<!-- <title>Determine derivative quadratic graphically</title> -->

<setup>

<selectFromSequence name="a" from="-1" to=" 1" step=" 0.2" exclude="0" />
<selectFromSequence name="b" from="-6abs($a)" to="6abs($a)" />
<number name="v">-$b /(2 $a)</number>
<round name="vhp_round" numDecimals="1">$a*$v^2+$b*$v</round>

<selectFromSequence name="c" from="-3-$vhp_round" to="3-$vhp_round" step=" 0.1" />
<number name="vh">$a *$v ^2+$b *$v +$c </number>

<function name="f">$a x^2 + $b x + $c</function>
<derivative name="fprime" symbolic="true">$f</derivative>
<function name="negfprime">-$$fprime(x)</function>
<line name="zeroline" equation="y=0" />
<line name="vertline" equation="x=$v+0.001y" />
  

<conditionalContent name="left_trend">
  <case condition="$a > 0">
    <text>decreasing</text>
    <text>increasing</text>
    <text>negative</text>
    <text>positive</text>
  </case>
  <else>
    <text>increasing</text>
    <text>decreasing</text>
    <text>positive</text>
    <text>negative</text>
  </else>
</conditionalContent>

<!-- <conditionalContent assignNames="(pointAtCenter)" maximumNumberToShow="1"> -->
<conditionalContent name="pointAtCenter">
  <case condition="$p1=$zero">
    <number>1</number>
  </case>
  <case condition="$p2=$zero">
    <number>2</number>
  </case>
  <case condition="$p3=$zero">
    <number>3</number>
  </case>
</conditionalContent>

</setup>


The <copy prop="styleDescription" source="f" /> parabola, below, is the graph of the quadratic function <m>f</m>.  (Quadratic means <m>f(x)=ax^2+bx+c</m> for some parameters <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>.)  Using the three <copy prop="styleDescription" source="p1" /> points, manipulate the  <copy prop="styleDescription" source="g" /> curve so that it is the graph of the derivative <m>f'(x)</m> of <m>f(x)</m>.

  
<graph size="medium" xmin="-5" xmax="5" ymin="-5" ymax="5" identicalAxisScales="true" grid="true">

<point hide="true" name="zero">($v, 0)</point>

<copy source="f" />

<point name="p1" x="-1" y="0" stylenumber="2">

  <conditionalContent>
    <case condition="$pointAtCenter[1][1]=2 or $pointAtCenter[1][1]=3">
      <attractTo threshold="1">
        $fprime$negfprime$zeroline$vertline
      </attractTo>
    </case>
    <else>
      <attractTo threshold="0.1">
        $fprime$negfprime$zeroline$vertline
      </attractTo>
      <attractTo threshold="0.3">$zero</attractTo>
    </else>
  </conditionalContent>

</point>

<point name="p2" x="0" y="0" stylenumber="2">

  <conditionalContent>
    <case condition="$pointAtCenter[1][1]=1 or $pointAtCenter[1][1]=3">
      <attractTo threshold="1">
        $fprime$negfprime$zeroline$vertline
      </attractTo>
    </case>
    <else>
      <attractTo threshold="0.1">
        $fprime$negfprime$zeroline$vertline
      </attractTo>
      <attractTo threshold="0.3">$zero</attractTo>
    </else>
  </conditionalContent>

</point>

<point name="p3" x="1" y="0" stylenumber="2">

  <conditionalContent>
    <case condition="$pointAtCenter[1][1]=1 or $pointAtCenter[1][1]=2">
      <attractTo threshold="1">
        $fprime$negfprime$zeroline$vertline
      </attractTo>
    </case>
    <else>
      <attractTo threshold="0.1">
        $fprime$negfprime$zeroline$vertline
      </attractTo>
      <attractTo threshold="0.3">$zero</attractTo>
    </else>
  </conditionalContent>

</point>

<parabola through="$p1 $p2 $p3" stylenumber="2" name="g" />

</graph>

<answer>
<award><when>
$$fprime(<copy source="p1" prop="x" />) = <copy source="p1" prop="y" /> and
$$fprime(<copy source="p2" prop="x" />) = <copy source="p2" prop="y" /> and
$$fprime(<copy source="p3" prop="x" />) = <copy source="p3" prop="y" />
</when></award>
</answer>

<solution>

<round hide="true" name="vx" numDecimals="3">$v</round>

<p>The function <m>f(x)</m> is $left_trend[1][1]  for <m>x \lt $vx </m>.  This means the derivative <m>f'(x)</m> is $left_trend[1][3]  for <m>x \lt  $vx </m>.  At <m>x=$vx </m>, the <m>f(x)</m> is horizontal, meaning the derivative is <m>f'($vx )</m> is zero.  The function <m>f(x)</m> is $left_trend[1][2]  for <m>x \gt $vx </m>.  This means the derivative <m>f'(x)</m> is $left_trend[1][4]  for <m>x \gt  $vx </m>.  The resulting graph of <m>f'(x)</m> is shown below.</p>

<graph size="medium" xmin="-5" xmax="5" ymin="-5" ymax="5" identicalAxisScales="true" grid="true">

<copy source="f" />
<copy source="fprime" stylenumber="2" />
<point stylenumber="2" fixed="true">($v, 0)</point>
<point stylenumber="2" fixed="true">($v-1, $$fprime($v-1))</point>
<point stylenumber="2" fixed="true">($v+1, $$fprime($v+1))</point>

</graph>


</solution>


</problem>